WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.307
Most examples in this module depend on the same relationship.

00:00:04.307 --> 00:00:10.062
A patient can have many admissions, many lab results, and many prescriptions.

00:00:10.062 --> 00:00:16.982
When you see a correlated condition such as a dot patient ID equals p dot patient ID,

00:00:16.982 --> 00:00:20.782
it is connecting a child row to the current patient row.

00:00:20.782 --> 00:00:28.737
Keeping the relationship map simple lets us focus on the subquery behavior rather than on a complicated

00:00:28.737 --> 00:00:34.917
schema. Connect this idea to a compliance workflow checking whether related evidence exists.

00:00:34.917 --> 00:00:40.832
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:40.832 --> 00:00:48.957
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:48.957 --> 00:00:55.277
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:55.277 --> 00:01:01.097
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
